---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout 
  title="How to Run a .py File Online and on Windows | Step-by-Step Guide"
  description="Learn how to execute .py Python files in your browser and on Windows systems. Covers browser file uploads, PowerShell commands, and virtual environments."
>
  <Navbar />

  <main class="flex-1 w-full bg-[var(--g-canvas)] py-10">
    <article class="max-w-[860px] mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
      
      <!-- Breadcrumb Navigation -->
      <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-mono text-[var(--g-mute)] mb-6">
        <a href="/" class="hover:text-[var(--g-ink)] transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span class="text-[var(--g-body)]">Guides</span>
        <span>/</span>
        <span class="text-[var(--g-ink)]">How to run a .py file</span>
      </nav>

      <!-- Article Header -->
      <header class="pb-8 border-b border-[var(--g-hairline)] mb-10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
          <span class="text-[11px] font-mono px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-[var(--g-link-soft)] text-[var(--color-brand-violet)] border border-[#e9d5ff] font-medium">
            Execution guide
          </span>
          <span class="text-xs text-[var(--g-mute)] font-mono">Updated October 2026</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-[var(--g-mute)] font-mono">•</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-[var(--g-mute)] font-mono">6 min read</span>
        </div>
        
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.04em] text-[var(--g-ink)] leading-tight">
          How to run a .py file online and in Windows terminal
        </h1>

        <p class="text-base text-[var(--g-body)] mt-4 leading-relaxed">
          A .py file contains plain-text Python source code. You can execute this file directly inside a browser through WebAssembly file mounting, or on a Windows workstation using PowerShell and the Python launcher.
        </p>

        <!-- Quick Launch CTA Card -->
        <div class="mt-6 p-4 rounded-[10px] bg-[var(--g-elevated)] border border-[var(--g-hairline)] shadow-[var(--g-shadow-sm)] flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center justify-between gap-4">
          <div>
            <div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--g-ink)]">Have a .py file to run?</div>
            <div class="text-xs text-[var(--g-mute)] mt-0.5">Upload and execute your file in the browser runner without installing Python.</div>
          </div>
          <a 
            href="/" 
            class="h-9 px-4 btn-primary text-xs font-medium rounded-full flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5 transition-all shadow-sm shrink-0"
          >
            <span>Upload and run .py file</span>
            <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path d="M21 15v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2v-4"></path>
              <polyline points="17 8 12 3 7 8"></polyline>
              <line x1="12" y1="3" x2="12" y2="15"></line>
            </svg>
          </a>
        </div>
      </header>

      <!-- Article Body -->
      <div class="space-y-10 text-sm text-[var(--g-body)] leading-relaxed">

        <!-- Section 1 -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-3">
            Running a .py file online through browser file upload
          </h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Online runners allow you to test scripts without configuring operating system paths. When you click the Upload button in the workspace tab strip on python-code-runner.com, the browser uses the HTML5 FileReader API to read the text content of your .py file into browser memory.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            The runner writes this text to the Pyodide virtual file system under the root directory as /filename.py. You can upload multiple files such as main.py and data.json into the same virtual file system. When you execute main.py, relative imports locate helper files in the same virtual working directory.
          </p>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--g-canvas)] border border-[var(--g-hairline)] rounded-[8px] font-mono text-xs text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-4">
            <div class="text-[var(--g-mute)] mb-1"># Example of multi-file relative import in WebAssembly VFS</div>
            <div># File: main.py</div>
            <div>import helper</div>
            <div>result = helper.calculate_vat(100.0, 0.20)</div>
            <div>{'print(f"Calculated VAT total: {result}") # prints: 120.0'}</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 2 -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-3">
            Running a .py file locally on Windows via PowerShell
          </h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Windows requires a local CPython interpreter installation. Download the official 64-bit installer for Python 3.12 from python.org. Check the box labeled "Add python.exe to PATH" during installation. This option writes the directory path C:\Users\&lt;username&gt;\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python312 into your user PATH environment variable.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Open PowerShell by pressing Windows Key + R, typing powershell, and pressing Enter. Change directory to the folder containing your script using the Set-Location command:
          </p>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--g-canvas)] border border-[var(--g-hairline)] rounded-[8px] font-mono text-xs text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-4">
            <div class="text-[var(--g-mute)] mb-1"># Navigate to directory and execute</div>
            <div>cd C:\Users\kamlesh\Desktop\my-python-project</div>
            <div>python script.py</div>
          </div>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Windows also includes the Python launcher binary named py.exe. When you have multiple Python versions installed, such as Python 3.11 and Python 3.12, py.exe allows you to select the runtime explicitly:
          </p>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--g-canvas)] border border-[var(--g-hairline)] rounded-[8px] font-mono text-xs text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-4">
            <div class="text-[var(--g-mute)] mb-1"># Targeting specific Python versions with the Windows launcher</div>
            <div>py -3.12 script.py</div>
            <div>py -3.11 script.py</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 3 -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-3">
            Creating isolated virtual environments with venv
          </h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Installing libraries globally with pip can cause version conflicts across different software projects. The Python standard library provides the venv module to create self-contained directory trees.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Run the following three commands in PowerShell to create and activate a project environment:
          </p>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--g-canvas)] border border-[var(--g-hairline)] rounded-[8px] font-mono text-xs text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-4">
            <div class="text-[var(--g-mute)] mb-1"># Virtual environment setup on Windows</div>
            <div>python -m venv .venv</div>
            <div>.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1</div>
            <div>pip install requests==2.31.0</div>
            <div>python main.py</div>
          </div>
          <p class="mb-4">
            If PowerShell blocks the activation script with an execution policy error, run Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser to permit locally created scripts.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 4 -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-3">
            Resolving common Windows file execution errors
          </h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Three errors occur frequently when executing .py files on Windows workstations.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-2">Error 1: "python is not recognized as an internal or external command"</h3>
          <p class="mb-4">
            This message means the directory containing python.exe is missing from the system PATH variable. Run the installer again, choose Modify, and select the checkbox for environment variable registration.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-2">Error 2: "No such file or directory"</h3>
          <p class="mb-4">
            This error occurs when PowerShell runs in a different folder than the script file. Verify your current directory with the Get-Location command (or pwd) and verify the file presence with Get-ChildItem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-2">Error 3: Script window closes immediately after execution</h3>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Double-clicking a .py file in Windows File Explorer opens a temporary console window that closes as soon as the last line finishes. To inspect output, run the script from an active PowerShell session, or append input("Press Enter to exit...") to the end of your script.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Bottom CTA -->
        <section class="p-6 rounded-[12px] bg-[var(--g-canvas)] border border-[var(--g-hairline)] text-center mt-12">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-2">Execute your .py file in the browser</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-[var(--g-mute)] max-w-lg mx-auto mb-4">
            Upload your Python script to python-code-runner.com to test code, inspect variables, and view output without local software configuration.
          </p>
          <a 
            href="/" 
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 h-9 px-5 btn-primary text-xs font-semibold rounded-full transition-all shadow-sm"
          >
            <span>Open Python Code Runner</span>
            <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line>
              <polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline>
            </svg>
          </a>
        </section>

      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
